    <!-- 1. Core Features -->
    <section id="core-features">
      <h2>Core Virtual Tabletop Features For Live Play</h2>
      <p>
        The goal is to help the GM move fast without turning the session into a slow video-game interface. Open the map,
        move tokens, mark the scene, jump between linked tables, and keep players focused on the fiction.
      </p>
      <figure class="toolshot-card toolshot-card--focus">
        <img
          src="/assets/screen_shots/vtt-screenshot.png"
          alt="Virtual tabletop with battle map, tokens, drawing marks, and sidebar controls."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Maps, tokens, layers, drawing, chat, and table controls in one browser-based session surface.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fast-loading browser play</strong> - built for quick joins, efficient scene changes, and less waiting at the table.</li>
        <li><strong>Token controls</strong> - move, resize, rotate, lock, unlock, and manage visibility.</li>
        <li><strong>Map, object, and fog layers</strong> - separate terrain, tactical pieces, and translucent overlay marks.</li>
        <li><strong>Grid controls</strong> - show, hide, and resize the grid for different encounter scales.</li>
        <li><strong>Drawing tools</strong> - annotate scenes, mark spell areas, or sketch quick tactical changes mid-session.</li>
        <li><strong>Chat and dice</strong> - keep table messages and quick rolls close to the map.</li>
        <li><strong>Location pins and portals</strong> - mark key positions and jump between linked maps without losing pace.</li>
        <li><strong>Bring your own assets</strong> - upload your own maps and tokens, or keep reusable images organized in folders.</li>
        <li><strong>Free token starter pack and community packs</strong> - start with the free pack, then use Library Packs in Pro if you want installable community collections.</li>
        <li><strong>Template system (Pro)</strong> - save repeatable table setups for recurring encounters.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="dnd-5e">
      <h2>Virtual Tabletop For D&amp;D 5E Encounters And Maps</h2>
      <p>
        If your group mainly plays D&amp;D 5E, this workflow is built to keep encounters moving and support theater-of-the-mind pacing
        without burying the table under slow automation.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Run maps and tokens live</strong> - stage battles, dungeon reveals, and movement directly in the browser.</li>
        <li><strong>Handle combat quickly</strong> - use layers, visibility controls, grid sizing, chat dice, and pins in one place.</li>
        <li><strong>Connect adjacent tools</strong> - pair the table with <a href="/dnd-5e-character-sheets">5E character sheets</a>, the <a href="/resources/srd">SRD compendium</a>, and <a href="/wyrlds">Wyrlds</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>See the D&amp;D-focused overview</strong> - read <a href="/virtual-tabletop-for-dnd-5e">Virtual Tabletop for D&amp;D 5E</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="fast-setup">
      <h2>Fast Start For One-Shots And Low-Friction Groups</h2>
      <p>
        Setup speed still matters. When you need to get a table live quickly, players can join by link on simple tables
        and you can move from first click to playable scene without a long onboarding path.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Launch quickly</strong> - start a guest sandbox or full table in a few clicks.</li>
        <li><strong>Share one join link</strong> - players can enter without signup friction on simple table flows.</li>
        <li><strong>Pick your mode</strong> - use Standard for full tools or Sandbox for focused rehearsal.</li>
        <li><strong>Stay in session flow</strong> - less setup overhead before the first encounter.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- 3. GM Session Control -->
    <section id="gm-control">
      <h2>GM Control Without Slowing the Session</h2>
      <p>
        Keep encounters moving with clear table-state controls instead of adding slow, distracting overhead to the scene.
      </p>
      <figure class="toolshot-card">
        <img
          src="/assets/screen_shots/vtt_secret_tunnel.png"
          alt="VTT encounter scene with cave map, player tokens, and overlay markings."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Live encounter control: track token positioning, manage visibility, and keep pacing tight during tactical scenes.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Object visibility controls</strong> - hide or reveal pieces as scenes evolve.</li>
        <li><strong>Layer and ordering tools</strong> - keep tactical information readable during combat.</li>
        <li><strong>Pin and portal management</strong> - locate, restore, or clean up map markers and linked destinations quickly.</li>
        <li><strong>Role-aware permissions</strong> - managers keep full controls while participants interact in real time.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- 4. Campaign Depth -->
    <section id="campaign-depth">
      <h2>Scale from One-Shots to Persistent Campaigns</h2>
      <p>
        When a group becomes long-term, move into Wyrlds so campaign state stays organized between sessions. Wyrlds are
        invite-based campaign workspaces where players make accounts, link character sheets, and keep shared resources attached
        to the same group.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Linked multi-map play</strong> - connect rooms, regions, and encounter maps with location pins and portals.</li>
        <li><strong>Persistent records and calendars</strong> - keep lore, NPC notes, timelines, and session history in one campaign space.</li>
        <li><strong>Account-based campaign membership</strong> - invited players link character sheets and return to the same shared tools between sessions.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Need campaign management? Explore <a href="/wyrlds">Wyrlds</a>. Recruiting new players? Use
        <a href="/public-wyrlds-guide">Public Wyrlds</a> and <a href="/play-dungeons-and-dragons-online">Play D&amp;D Online</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 5. Competitive Search Intent -->
    <section id="alternatives">
      <h2>Need an Alternative to Roll20 or Owlbear Rodeo?</h2>
      <p>
        Far Reach Co is built for groups that want a fast, readable tabletop that supports theater of the mind instead of simulating every effect like a video game.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fast onboarding</strong> - invite-link joining and no-signup sandbox testing.</li>
        <li><strong>Fast-loading sessions</strong> - a lighter browser workflow with less waiting and less interface clutter.</li>
        <li><strong>Practical GM tools</strong> - layered controls, drawing, chat/dice, location pins, portals, and templates.</li>
        <li><strong>Campaign continuity</strong> - persistent <a href="/wyrlds">Wyrlds</a> when your one-shot grows into a full arc.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If your main priority is dynamic-lighting-heavy simulation, that is not the design center here. The focus is speed,
        clarity, and immersion with fewer distractions.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a direct breakdown of where this fits best, read <a href="/roll20-alternative">our Roll20 alternative page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

